## Where I think the reviewer is wrong (NOT applied)
1. b#14 (and the date-order half of a#42): "Enero 24, 2022 is US month-day order; write 24 Enero 2022."
   Philippine usage — including this corpus's own unchangeable frontmatter `date_display: "Setyembre 13, 2025"` /
   "Setyembre 17, 2026" — is month-day-year. "24 Enero 2022" is neither the PH convention nor idiomatic Filipino
   (the formal Filipino form would be "ika-24 ng Enero 2022"). I fixed only the genuine violation the rulebook names
   (ISO dates in running text, a#42) and normalized them to the corpus's month-day-year form.
2. a#39's "hindi ito 911 ng Pilipinas": injects a country term into a block whose Korean source names no country
   (checker `nationality` lane FAILs) plus an unsourced digit. R9's Taiwan labelling was applied instead.
3. a#59's proposed title with "(huwag itumbas sa separation pay sa Labor Code ng Pilipinas)": same nationality risk,
   and it is a page <title>. The caveat was put in the body's first mention of 資遣費 instead.
4. a#44 / b#12's full replacement of "first instance"/"second instance" with Filipino-only wording: the checker's fil
   ordinal lane recognizes only the English tokens, so removing them drops the Korean 1심/2심 digits (FAIL).
   Filipino glosses were added alongside instead; the 010 title (no ordinal lane pressure there) did take the Filipino form.

